{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "zebra_puzzle:fe37c4db2b25d85eb28bc39dc6c978c002216c9e26227d7a9389abad0dd230f0", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Let's analyze the problem step by step.\n\nWe have two people in a line: Person 1 (left) and Person 2 (right).\n\nEach person has three attributes:\n- Movie-Genre: crime or animation\n- Pet: ferret or lizard\n- Transport: tram or jet-ski\n\nEach attribute is unique for each person. That means:\n- No two people share the same movie-genre.\n- No two people share the same pet.\n- No two people share the same transport.\n\nConstraints:\n1. The person who watches **animation** is on the **immediate right** of the person who has a **ferret**.\n   This means:\n   - If Person 1 has the ferret, then Person 2 watches animation.\n   - If Person 2 has the ferret, there is no one to their immediate right, so this cannot be true. Therefore, the ferret must be with Person 1.\n\n2. The person who watches **crime** is on the **immediate left or immediate right** of the person who travels by **jet-ski**.\n   Since there are only two people, this means:\n   - The person who watches crime is either directly left or directly right of the jet-ski user.\n   - In terms of positions:\n     - If Person 1 uses jet-ski, Person 2 must watch crime (immediate right).\n     - If Person 2 uses jet-ski, Person 1 must watch crime (immediate left).\n\nLet's assign based on the first constraint:\nFrom constraint 1: Person 1 has the ferret, so Person 2 watches animation.\n\nNow, let's assign movie genres:\n- Person 2 watches animation → Person 1 must watch crime (since genres are unique).\n\nNow assign transports based on constraint 2:\n- The person who watches crime (Person 1) is on the immediate left or right of the person who travels by jet-ski.\n  - Since Person 1 is on the left of Person 2, Person 2 must be the one who uses the jet-ski (because Person 1 is immediately left of Person 2).\n 